    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Choose a factorization $K \rightarrow K' \rightarrow \bHom _{S}(S,X)$ of $p$, where $K \rightarrow K'$ is inner anodyne (and therefore a categorical equivalence) and $K' \rightarrow \calC ^ S$ is an inner fibration (so that $K'$ is an $\infty $-category). The map $K \rightarrow K'$ is a categorical equivalence, and therefore cofinal. We are free to replace $K$ by $K'$, and may thereby assume that $K$ is an $\infty $-category. 
    <p>
      We apply Proposition <a href="/tag/C3F034" data-tag="C3F034">4.2.2.7</a> to the Cartesian fibration $X \rightarrow S$ and the diagram $p_ S: K \times S \rightarrow X$ determined by the map $p$. We deduce that there exists a map 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[ \overline{p}_ S: (K \times S) \diamond _ S S = (K \diamond \Delta ^0) \times S \rightarrow X \]
</div>
    <p>
       having the property that its restriction to the fiber over each $s \in S$ is a colimit of $p_ s$; this proves $(1)$. 
    <p>
      The “if” direction of $(2)$ follows immediately from Lemma <a href="/tag/93B068" data-tag="93B068">5.1.2.1</a>. The “only if” follows from $(1)$ and the fact that colimits, when they exist, are unique up to equivalence. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
